## 14. Separate Authentication for Users and Dietitians

Implement complete sign-up, login, logout, password reset, session handling, and profile management. The User and Dietitian experiences must be clearly separate from the first authentication screen onward. Do not use one generic registration form for both roles.

### First authentication screen
Show two clear options
- Continue as User — for people scanning food, exploring recipes, planning meals, or finding a dietitian.
- Continue as Dietitian — for qualified or newly qualified dietitians who want to create a professional profile and manage clients.

Each option must open its own login and sign-up flow. Include a clear way to return to role selection or switch roles. Keep the existing WhatsInit design system and make the flows responsive on mobile and desktop.

### User sign-up and onboarding
Collect only the information needed to provide the service
- Full name
- Email address
- Password
- Optional age or age range
- Dietary preferences
- Allergies and intolerances
- Food budget and preferred serving count
- Optional goals such as general wellness or weight management
- Optional health information, such as diabetes or high blood pressure, only with clear consent

Allow users to skip optional health questions, edit preferences later, and delete their account. Health information must remain private and must never appear on a public profile.

After successful user registration, direct the user to the User Dashboard and user onboarding flow. Users should see user features such as food scanning, nutrition reports, recipes, meal planning, grocery lists, scan history, and dietitian discovery.

### Dietitian sign-up and onboarding
Use a separate professional registration form with
- Full name
- Email address
- Password
- Qualification and institution
- Specializations or areas of expertise
- Years of experience, if any
- Languages spoken
- Consultation fee
- Availability and preferred consultation format
- Professional registration or credential details and supporting documents, where applicable
- Short professional bio

Do not invent or automatically approve qualifications. Store submitted credentials securely and show a clear status such as Pending Verification, Verified, or Additional Information Required. Display a verified badge only after the platform's actual verification process succeeds. Do not publicly expose private documents or sensitive personal information.

After registration, direct dietitians to a Dietitian Dashboard where they can complete their profile, manage availability, review booking requests, manage client appointments, and upload meal-plan documents when the relevant feature is configured. Clearly distinguish a free basic profile from any paid professional plan.

### Role-based access and security
- Store each account's role securely as `user` or `dietitian`.
- Enforce role permissions on the serverbackend as well as in the interface; hiding a page or button alone is not authorization.
- Users must not be able to access dietitian-only dashboards or private client records.
- Dietitians must not be able to access another dietitian's private records or a user's account without an authorized booking or appropriate consent.
- Validate inputs, hash passwords with a secure standard, protect sessions, and implement safe password-reset flows.
- Show useful validation errors and loadingsuccess states. Prevent duplicate registrations for the same email.
- If a real backend or authentication provider is not configured, clearly label any demo authentication as demo-only; do not pretend that accounts are securely stored or authenticated in production.

Do not make sensitive health information publicly visible. Provide account settings for editing profile information, changing a password, and requesting account deletion.

## 15. Recommended Technical Architecture

First inspect the existing project structure and preserve working code. If the repository is empty, create a suitable project from scratch.

Recommended stack

- Frontend Next.js with TypeScript
- Styling Tailwind CSS
- UI components accessible reusable component library
- Backend Next.js API routes or a dedicated backend
- Database PostgreSQL
- Authentication secure session-based or token-based authentication
- Image handling secure uploads with file validation
- OCR a suitable OCR provider
- AI a configurable AI provider through server-side API calls
- Charts a suitable charting library
- Payments a provider supporting Indian payments, integrated only when credentials are configured

